# Load a frozen character fixture, photograph him, and save him again.
#
#   INCURSION_OPTIONS=tools/fixtures/options-2026-08-22.dat \
#   INCURSION_LOAD=tools/fixtures/chars/<name>.sav \
#       tools/headless.sh tools/keys/load-char-sheet.keys 1
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. It is the reading half of tools/fixtures/chars/: the script
# that turns a .sav back into something a person or a check can assert on. It
# builds no character of its own, so it works on ANY fixture -- the session
# starts in play mode with the saved character already standing on the saved
# map, because tools/headless.sh passed -load. Run it with no INCURSION_LOAD
# and there is no character, the game sits in its title menu, and the run
# reports NO GAMEPLAY rather than quietly measuring something else.
#
# Both users are deliberate. tools/make_char_fixture.sh runs it to write the
# <name>.sheet.txt half of a new fixture, which means a fixture is never
# published without having been loaded once. tools/check_char_fixture.sh runs
# it to assert that the character who comes back is the one the sheet claims.
#
# THE SEARCH IS NOT FILLER. tools/headless.sh reports NO GAMEPLAY (exit 5)
# unless a turn actually completes, and every assertion library in tools/
# refuses to read a session that measured nothing -- that is inc-loa.3. A
# loaded session that only opened menus would complete no turn at all, so the
# 's' below buys the one turn that makes this a session.
#
# THE SAVE AT THE END IS THE POINT OF THE WHOLE SCRIPT, not a courtesy. A
# loaded session owns its save file and writes back to it (Game::SaveGame,
# src/Registry.cpp:1114), so this is the one action that can prove the frozen
# fixture was copied into the sandbox rather than played in place. Read the
# warning in tools/keys/save-continue.keys before moving the ESC: ESC opens
# the System Menu rather than cancelling anything, [a] is Save and Quit, and a
# stray ESC therefore ends the run and still exits 0. The @expect is the guard.

@dump:arrival

s               # search: one real turn, so this counts as a session
@dump:played

d               # Display Character Sheet
@expect "Gender"
@dump:sheet
ESC             # leave the sheet, back to play mode

# ONE PAGE ONLY. PGDN scrolls the sheet by a single line, so a second dump
# would repeat the first and prove nothing. The full sheet -- feats, special
# abilities, the whole inventory, the journal -- reaches disk another way:
# INCURSION_CHAR_PROBE=1 makes Game::SaveGame write logs/charprobe.txt
# (src/Registry.cpp:1105-1111), untruncated by the 80x48 terminal, and that
# file is what tools/make_char_fixture.sh keeps as <name>.sheet.txt.

ESC                     # System Menu -- and NOT a cancel; see the header
@expect "System Menu"
b                       # [b] Save and Continue

# Fires after the save lands, and photographs the engine's own
# "Autosave...  Done." line, which is the oracle that it did.
@dump:saved

# No @quit: it is Save and Quit (src/Wposix.cpp:1657-1664) and would write a
# second save this script did not ask for. Running out of keys ends the
# session with exactly the one save above.
